## How big is one number?

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 small has about 124 million weights, and its files store each one in 4 bytes: about 500 MB
in all.<Cite id="gpt2-model-card" /> The same rule works for any model:

**memory for the weights ≈ number of weights × bits per weight ÷ 8 bytes**

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2 small has 124,439,808 weights. Its published files store each one as a 32-bit floating-point
number: 4 bytes (<ChapterRef to="text-is-numbers" />), enough for about seven significant digits.<Cite id="gpt2-model-card" /> That
comes to about 498 MB. The same rule works for any model:

**memory for the weights ≈ number of weights × bits per weight ÷ 8 bytes**

</At>

At 16 bits, GPT-2 small needs about 249 MB. The largest Llama 3 model from <ChapterRef to="scale" />, with 405 billion
weights, needs about 810 GB at 16 bits and about 203 GB at 4 bits. A smaller model is also quicker to
load and to read from memory.

<At levels="simple">

Does a weight need that much detail? Rounding each number to one of a few allowed values is called <GlossaryPopover term="quantization">quantization</GlossaryPopover>.
With 8 bits a number can take 256 values; with 4 bits, only 16. Slide GPT-2 small down from 32
bits and watch what it predicts after "The cat sat on the":

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Does a weight need seven digits? Storing numbers with fewer bits, by rounding each one to a small set
of allowed values, is called <GlossaryPopover term="quantization">quantization</GlossaryPopover>.
With 8 bits a number can take 256 different values; with 4 bits, 16; with 2 bits, only 4. To make
those few values count, each block of 32 weights shares one scale, set by its largest number, and
each weight is stored as a small whole number times that scale. File formats for sharing models,
like GGUF's Q4_0 and Q8_0, work this way.<Cite id="gguf-quant-types" /> Slide GPT-2 small down from
32 bits and watch what it predicts after "The cat sat on the":

</At>

<QuantizeLab />

<At levels="simple">

At 16 and 8 bits nothing visible changes. At 6 and 5 bits the guesses drift, but stay sensible. At
4 bits and below, the model falls apart and writes gibberish.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

At 16 and 8 bits nothing visible changes: the same top guess, " floor", the same next twelve tokens,
and almost the same loss (<ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />) on a test passage. At 6 and 5 bits the guesses drift: " bed", then
" table", still sensible. At 4 bits and below the model falls apart, repeating "The stack." or
writing gibberish, and its loss shoots up. Look at the dots: as the bits run out, each weight snaps
to a coarser grid, and the small weights in a block with one large weight are rounded hardest.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Block rounding, written down

For a block of 32 weights $w_1, \dots, w_{32}$ whose largest size is $m$, $n$-bit rounding works
like this:

$$
s = \frac{m}{2^{\,n-1} - 1}, \qquad q_i = \operatorname{round}\!\left(\frac{w_i}{s}\right), \qquad \hat{w}_i = s\, q_i
$$

where $s$ is the block's scale (stored once), $q_i$ is the small whole number stored for weight $i$,
and $\hat{w}_i$ is the value the model uses. Each weight moves by at most $s/2$. In the block the
widget shows, $m = 0.689$, so at 4 bits the step $s$ is about 0.098, and every weight smaller than
0.049 becomes exactly 0. That's why the small weights suffer most.

The scale costs 16 bits per block, half a bit per weight, so "4-bit" really means about 4.5 bits
per weight: GPT-2 small at 4 bits takes 70 MB, not 62. GGUF's Q4_0 stores exactly this, 4-bit
numbers plus one 16-bit scale per 32.<Cite id="gguf-quant-types" />

</At>

You have been using a quantized model all along. The live GPT-2 on this site runs with 8-bit
weights, a 128 MB download instead of about 500 MB. It was rounded by a different method than the
demo's, and its percentages differ from the recorded ones: it gives " floor" 15.4% instead of 7.6%.

## Rounding with care

<At levels="simple">

This demo rounds in the plainest way. Better methods keep more quality with fewer bits. One lets
the model practice with the rounding during training, called <GlossaryPopover term="quantization-aware-training">quantization-aware training</GlossaryPopover>
(QAT). Google's Gemma 4 QAT models use 4-bit weights, and their model card describes their quality
as similar to the 16-bit originals.<Cite id="gemma4-qat" /> That is what the `qat` in a model name
means.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

This demo uses the plainest method, rounding to the nearest allowed value. Better methods keep more
quality at fewer bits. Large transformers have a few unusually large numbers that plain rounding
handles badly; treating them separately lets 8-bit versions match 16-bit ones even at 175 billion
weights.<Cite id="dettmers2022" /> Another way is to let the model learn to cope with the rounding.
With <GlossaryPopover term="quantization-aware-training">quantization-aware training</GlossaryPopover>
(QAT), the weights are rounded during training too, so training nudges them (<ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />) toward values that
still work after rounding.<Cite id="jacob2018" /> Google's Gemma 4 models come in QAT versions with
4-bit weights, and their model card describes their quality as similar to the 16-bit
originals.<Cite id="gemma4-qat" /> That is what the `qat` in a model name means.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

In QAT, the forward pass uses the rounded weights $\hat{w}$, so the loss includes the rounding
error, while backpropagation treats rounding as if it weren't there and updates the full-precision
weights $w$. Rounding has a slope of 0 almost everywhere, so without that shortcut no weight would
ever move.<Cite id="jacob2018" /> The outlier method instead finds the few feature dimensions with
very large values and multiplies them in 16 bits, and everything else in 8.<Cite id="dettmers2022" />

</At>

A small model like GPT-2 has less to spare than a big one: larger models are generally easier to
round.<Cite id="frantar2023" /> And this demo rounds everything, including the token table. So read
the 4-bit result as "plain rounding has limits", not "4 bits is too few".

## One bit per weight

<At levels="simple">

Rounding a finished model has a floor. A hobby project rounded a 2-billion-weight model after
training: at 2 bits it still wrote fluently but said Australia's capital is Canterbury and repeated
itself, and at 1 bit it stopped answering at all.<Cite id="smolmaxxing" />

A model that learns with very few values from the start does far better. BitNet b1.58 lets every
weight be only −1, 0, or 1 while it trains, and from 3 billion weights up it matched a 16-bit model
of the same size.<Cite id="ma2024bitnet" /> Bonsai 8B stores every weight as a single bit: 8
billion weights in 1.15 GB instead of 16.4 GB.<Cite id="bonsai-8b-model-card" /> It runs on a TV box
with about 2 GB of memory.<Cite id="smolmaxxing" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

How few bits can a weight get? Rounding a finished model has a floor. With plain rounding, the
method in the demo above, the GPTQ paper's models collapsed at 3 bits.<Cite id="frantar2023" /> A
hobby project, smolmaxxing, rounded the blocks of Qwen3.5-2B after training. At about 2 bits it
still wrote fluently, but said Australia's capital is Canterbury and fell into loops. At 1 bit,
where each weight is only a sign times its block's scale, its perplexity (<ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />) went from about 9
to over 75,000, and it answered nothing.<Cite id="smolmaxxing" />

Quantization-aware training taken to the extreme does much better. BitNet b1.58 is trained from
scratch with every weight restricted to −1, 0, or 1, and from 3 billion weights up it matched a
16-bit model of the same size trained on the same data, in perplexity and in benchmark
scores.<Cite id="ma2024bitnet" /> Bonsai 8B, which PrismML built from Qwen3-8B with a method it hasn't
published, stores every weight as a single bit, in the same 1-bit format that destroyed the rounded
model: 8.19 billion weights in 1.15 GB instead of 16.4 GB at 16 bits. Its makers report benchmark
scores matching full-precision 8-billion-weight models.<Cite id="bonsai-8b-model-card" /> smolmaxxing
runs it on a TV box with about 2 GB of memory at about 5 tokens a second.<Cite id="smolmaxxing" /> The
bits a model can live with depend on how it was made, not only on its size.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Three values, written down

Three values need $\log_2 3 \approx 1.58$ bits each, hence the name. BitNet b1.58 rounds a weight
matrix $W$ by its average size $\gamma$, the mean of $|w|$ over the matrix:

$$\widetilde{W} = \operatorname{clip}\!\left(\operatorname{round}\!\left(\frac{W}{\gamma}\right), -1, 1\right)$$

so every weight becomes −1, 0, or 1, with $\gamma$ kept as the matrix's one scale. Multiplying by
−1, 0, or 1 needs almost no multiplications, only adding, subtracting, or skipping.<Cite id="ma2024bitnet" /> Bonsai's format keeps one 16-bit scale per block of 128 one-bit
weights, $1 + 16/128 = 1.125$ bits per weight, which is how 8.19 billion weights fit in
1.15 GB.<Cite id="bonsai-8b-model-card" />

</At>

## Checking a rounded model

<At levels="simple">

The demo judges each precision by its loss on a test passage. That score checks each next token
with the real text before it, but when the model writes, it reads its own words, and small slips
can pile up.<Cite id="ranzato2016" /> So a good score can hide a broken model. smolmaxxing rounded
part of an 8-billion-weight model to 2 bits: its score on test text got somewhat worse, but
every one of 7 long answers fell into a loop.<Cite id="smolmaxxing" /> Test a rounded model the way
you'll use it: let it write, and read what it writes.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The demo judges each precision by its loss on a test passage, as most comparisons do. But the loss
scores each next token with the real text before it. When the model writes, it reads its own
output instead, and small slips can pile up.<Cite id="ranzato2016" /> A score on clean text can't
see that. smolmaxxing rounded the largest part of Moon-Beam-8B-A1B, an 8-billion-weight model, to
2 bits. Its perplexity on test text went from 10.1 at 4 bits to 11.8, but on the TV box all 7 of
its long answers, about 1,000 tokens each, fell into loops; with 8-bit weights, run on a Mac, none
of 6 did.<Cite id="smolmaxxing" /> In the setup it used every day, 12 of 13 long answers looped, and a
penalty for repeated tokens (<ChapterRef to="generating-text" />) brought that down to 1. So test a rounded model the way you'll use it: let it
write long answers, and read them.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Memory for the weights is about the number of weights × bits per weight ÷ 8 bytes.
- Quantization rounds each weight to one of a few allowed values, shared in blocks with one scale.
- GPT-2 small is unchanged at 8 bits, drifts at 6 and 5, and breaks at 4 with plain rounding.
- Better methods, like quantization-aware training, keep quality at 4 bits.
- Rounding a finished model to 1 or 2 bits breaks it, but models made for it work with weights of
  only −1, 0, or 1, or a single bit.
- A loss on test text can look fine while the model's own long answers fall apart; test it by
  letting it write.

<At levels="deep">

- A block's scale is $s = m / (2^{n-1} - 1)$ and each weight stores $\operatorname{round}(w / s)$:
  about 4.5 bits per weight at 4 bits.
- Three values take $\log_2 3 \approx 1.58$ bits; one bit plus a 16-bit scale per 128 weights is
  1.125.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** smaller numbers make a model cheaper to store and read. Could it also write
faster, without changing a single word?
